/**

@page page_exahype2_tutorials_limiting Part 4 - A 2D Euler Shock with a-posteriori Limiting

\tableofcontents

This tutorial adds the last big ingredient: a *limiter*. We solve a 2D
Riemann (shock-tube) problem for the compressible Euler equations: a corner of
low-density, low-pressure gas sits in a box of dense, high-pressure gas. When the
simulation starts, the dense gas rushes into the corner, launching **shock waves**
and **contact discontinuities**. The notebook is
`tutorials/04_limiting/EulerShock.ipynb`, with its implementation in
`ADERDGSolver.cpp`, `FVSolver.cpp` and `LimitingSolver.cpp`.

# Why a Limiter

A high-order ADER-DG scheme is accurate where the flow is smooth, but it
overshoots and undershoots near discontinuities (Gibbs oscillations). Those
oscillations can drive the density or pressure negative and crash the run. The
**a-posteriori limiter** keeps the scheme both accurate and robust:

- after each time step every cell is checked against a discrete maximum
  principle (DMP) and a user-supplied *physical admissibility criterion*;
- a cell that fails - a *troubled* cell - is rolled back and recomputed with a
  robust Finite Volumes scheme on a subgrid of `2 * order + 1` volumes;
- everywhere else the high-order ADER-DG solution is kept.

# The Equations

We solve the 2D compressible Euler equations for density \f$\rho\f$, momentum
\f$\rho \mathbf{u}\f$ and energy \f$E\f$. There is no source term. The conserved
variables are addressed through the generated `Shortcuts` enum (`Shortcuts::rho`,
`Shortcuts::j + 0/1`, `Shortcuts::E`), so the same indices work in all three
implementation files.

# The Configuration

The domain is the box \f$[-1, 1]^2\f$. The bottom-left quadrant starts with
\f$\rho = 0.125,\ p = 0.1\f$ and the rest of the box with \f$\rho = 1,\ p = 1\f$,
both at rest. The outer state never changes during the run, so the boundaries are
Dirichlet conditions that simply hold that initial outer state.

Both solvers solve the *same* equations, so we create a high-order `ADERDGSolver`
and a robust `FVSolver` with the same terms and wrap them in a `PosterioriLimiting`
solver. The limiter recomputes a cell with Finite Volumes whenever the discrete
maximum principle or the physical admissibility criterion flags it as troubled:

    aderdg_solver = exahype2.solvers.aderdg.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep(
        name = "ADERDGSolver", order = 5, ...
    )
    fv_solver = exahype2.solvers.fv.godunov.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep(
        name = "FVSolver", patch_size = 2 * 5 + 1, ...
    )
    limiter = exahype2.solvers.limiting.PosterioriLimiting(
        name                             = "LimitingSolver",
        regular_solver                   = aderdg_solver,
        limiting_solver                  = fv_solver,
        number_of_dmp_observables        = 4,
        dmp_relaxation_parameter         = 0.01,
        dmp_differences_scaling          = 0.03,
        physical_admissibility_criterion = exahype2.solvers.PDETerms.User_Defined_Implementation,
    )

# The Implementation Files

`ADERDGSolver.cpp` holds the Euler equations as a 2D Riemann problem. The vacuum
guard (`if (p > pZero)`) keeps the flux and eigenvalue well defined even when an
oscillation briefly pushes a cell to a non-physical state; the limiter then
recomputes that cell. `FVSolver.cpp` carries the identical physics, so it is not
repeated here.

@include tutorials/04_limiting/ADERDGSolver.cpp

`LimitingSolver.cpp` holds the troubled-cell test - the limiter calls it for every
cell and recomputes the ones it rejects with Finite Volumes. It reuses the
regular solver's `Shortcuts` and `NumberOfUnknowns`, so nothing is hardcoded:

@include tutorials/04_limiting/LimitingSolver.cpp

# Running and Visualising

    export OMP_NUM_THREADS=4
    ./EulerShock -et 2.0 -ns 20 -l 3

We plot the energy from the `ADERDGSolver` (use the field dropdown to switch to
density, momentum or pressure):

    from postprocessing import render_solution
    render_solution.show_solution("ADERDGSolver", field="E")

Drag the time slider to partway through the run, where the structure is clearest:

@image html documentation/exahype2/tutorials/Euler_shock_energy.png

The energy at \f$t \approx 0.8\f$: the curved shock front, the contact
discontinuities and the central jet of the 2D Riemann problem all stand out. The
limiter keeps the solution physical (positive density and pressure) right through
these discontinuities, quietly swapping troubled ADER-DG cells for Finite Volume
ones.

# Why We Need the Limiter

The limiter is not a cosmetic detail here - the scheme is unusable without it. In
the notebook, go back to the solver cell, set

    use_limiting = False

and re-run from there. Now only the high-order ADER-DG scheme runs, with no Finite
Volume fallback, and it oscillates uncontrollably at the shock fronts: within a
fraction of the run the density goes negative, the solution blows up (densities of
tens of thousands), the reported maximum eigenvalue explodes - past 900, versus
around 50 with the limiter - and the time step collapses, so the run diverges and
grinds to a halt. That is the whole point of a-posteriori limiting: for problems
with shocks, a high-order scheme on its own is not robust, and the limiter is what
makes it usable.

# Experiment

- Refine the mesh with `-l 4` for sharper shocks (slower).
- Loosen or tighten the limiter via `dmp_relaxation_parameter` and
  `dmp_differences_scaling`, and watch how many cells get limited.
- Move the initial discontinuity (`ShockPosition` in `ADERDGSolver.cpp`).

In @ref page_exahype2_tutorials_elastic you take ExaHyPE 2 to three dimensions -
3D elastic waves - and measure how the solver scales across parallel hardware.

*/
